Here's the full migration from ASP.NET WebForms to ASP.NET Core 8 MVC.

## Patterns Identified

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| `Response.End()` | `btnExport_Click` | Return `FileContentResult` |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | `btnRedirect_Click` | Return `RedirectResult` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `btnRedirect_Click` | Remove entirely |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
// ASP.NET Core 8 — ReportController.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using System.Text;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // Was: btnExport_Click with Response.Write + Response.End
    // Now: Return a FileResult — the framework handles headers and termination.
    [HttpPost("export")]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();
        var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);

        // Sets Content-Type and Content-Disposition automatically
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    // Was: btnRedirect_Click with Response.Redirect(url, true)
    // Now: Return RedirectResult — no Thread.Abort, no ThreadAbortException.
    [HttpPost("redirect")]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // ThreadAbortException catch block is gone — it was only needed
        // because Response.Redirect(url, true) called Thread.Abort internally.
        // In ASP.NET Core, Redirect() just returns a result object.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() { /* ... */ }
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Calling `Response.Body.Write()` then trying to redirect** | In Core, once you write to the response body, you can't change headers or redirect. Return `IActionResult` instead. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Don't replace the `ThreadAbortException` catch with `OperationCanceledException` catch-and-swallow. The old catch only existed because `Response.Redirect(url, true)` threw internally — that pattern is gone. |
| **`User.Identity` can be `null`** | In Core, `User.Identity` is nullable. Use `?.IsAuthenticated ?? true` or the `[Authorize]` attribute. |
| **Missing `Response.End()` equivalent** | There is none. Returning from the action method *is* the equivalent. The framework finalizes the response. |

## If you need streaming (large reports)

```csharp
[HttpGet("export-large")]
public async Task<IActionResult> ExportLarge(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    Response.ContentType = "text/csv";
    Response.Headers.ContentDisposition = "attachment; filename=report.csv";

    await foreach (var chunk in GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken))
    {
        await Response.Body.WriteAsync(
            Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(chunk), cancellationToken);
        await Response.Body.FlushAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    // No Response.End() needed — returning completes the response.
    return new EmptyResult();
}
```

Note: pass `CancellationToken` (bound from `HttpContext.RequestAborted` automatically) so that work stops if the client disconnects — this is the cooperative replacement for the old Thread.Abort-based request termination.
